>

  <!-- § 01 born as an estimate -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">01</span><h2>Born as an estimate</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">First principles</span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>GDP for a quarter appears about eight weeks after the quarter ends. That speed has a price. Nobody has counted everything by then; nobody ever counts everything. The first number is an estimate, assembled from listed-company results, government accounts, bank credit, freight, tax collections and a hundred other proxies. It is the best reading possible on the evidence available, and it will change.</p>
        <aside class="defbox">
          <div class="term">Estimate <span class="sub">a reconstruction · not a count</span></div>
          <p>A statistical rebuilding of the economy from partial data. The national accounts infer the total from indicators, surveys and administrative records, and carry the informal sector forward by extrapolation between surveys.</p>
          <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> a careful sketch made before all the evidence has arrived.</div>
        </aside>
        <aside class="defbox">
          <div class="term">Vintage <span class="sub">the edition of the number</span></div>
          <p>The version of a statistic as published on a given date. The GDP of one past quarter exists in several vintages: advance estimate, provisional, first revised, final. Each is labelled by when it was published, because the number itself keeps moving.</p>
          <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> always ask which edition of the number you are reading.</div>
        </aside>
        <p style="margin-top:1.4em">One part of the economy is extrapolated every single time. The informal sector, which employs most Indians, is not surveyed quarterly. It is carried forward. Early prints lean on formal-sector proxies instead: listed companies, bank flows, diesel, freight. If the informal economy moves differently from the formal one, the early print will not see it. We do not know, quarter by quarter, how the informal sector is doing. The later revisions are partly that ignorance being corrected as survey evidence arrives, years late.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note · i</span>
          <b>Pencil, not ink.</b> Treat every first GDP print as a sketch. The final version arrives about three years after the year it describes.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note · ii</span>
          <b>We do not know.</b> The informal sector is extrapolated, not measured, between surveys. Saying so plainly is the accurate sentence.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note · iii</span>
          <b>Speed trades against certainty.</b> An eight-week print is fast because it leans on proxies. A three-year print is slow because it waited for the evidence.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 02 the schedule -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">02</span><h2>The revision schedule</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">MoSPI · about three years</span></div>
    <p>GDP passes through the same hands several times, and each pass is better informed than the last. The schedule below is the one to keep in mind when a headline treats a first print as settled fact.</p>
    <div class="break ledger" style="margin-top:48px">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Stage</th><th>When</th><th>What changes</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="name">Advance estimates<small>a projection</small></td>
            <td class="chg">Early January, before the year ends</td>
            <td class="name">A full-year figure built from partial data, months still outstanding</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="name">Provisional estimates<small>the first full set</small></td>
            <td class="chg">End of May, after the year closes</td>
            <td class="name">The first complete set of quarterly and annual GDP for the year</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="name">First revised estimates<small>the accounts arrive</small></td>
            <td class="chg">The following January</td>
            <td class="name">Audited company results and fuller government accounts replace proxies</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="name">Second and third revisions<small>the surveys land</small></td>
            <td class="chg">One and two years later</td>
            <td class="name">Survey results and tax data settle the informal sector and the total</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="name">Final<small>the number stops moving</small></td>
            <td class="chg">About three years after the year</td>
            <td class="name">The vintage becomes history; later changes come only with a base revision</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top:40px">The CPI runs on the opposite rule. The inflation print is final on release and is never revised: no second edition, no quiet correction. Prices can be sampled in full within the month, so there is nothing left to revise. GDP trades accuracy for speed and then revises; the CPI spends the month on accuracy and stays put.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- § 03 misreads -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">03</span><h2>How to misread this concept</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li>
        <span class="no">i.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“A revision means the earlier number was a lie.”</h3>
          <p>No. It means the earlier number was earlier. A revision is new evidence doing its work: company filings, audits and surveys that did not exist when the first print was made. The honest unit of GDP is the vintage, not the headline.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">ii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“The first print is the truth, the rest is politics.”</h3>
          <p>The revision calendar is published in advance and runs on schedule, not on convenience. Each stage exists because a specific kind of evidence arrives at a specific time. A statistical office that never revised would be claiming a precision it cannot have.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“Revisions always move the number the same way.”</h3>
          <p>They do not. Revisions cut both ways and often offset: one quarter nudged up, another nudged down. What they reliably change is the level of certainty attached to the story, not the direction of the story itself.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div slot="prov-source">
    <h6>Source of record</h6>
    <p>Ministry of Statistics &amp; Programme Implementation, National Statistical Office. The revision stages described follow the published calendar of the National Accounts: advance estimates, provisional estimates, then revised estimates over roughly three years. The Consumer Price Index is final on release and carries no revision schedule.</p>
  </div>
  <div slot="prov-revision">
    <h6>Definitions &amp; honesty</h6>
    <p>Estimate, vintage and revision are used here in their statistical senses: a revision is a scheduled improvement on better data, not a correction of misconduct. On the informal sector this page says plainly what the data cannot: we do not know, quarter by quarter, how it is doing between surveys.</p>
    <p>Where the evidence runs out, the page stops rather than inventing precision.</p>
  </div>
  <div slot="prov-note">
    <h6>This note</h6>
    <p>Learn № 14 · Reading numbers · version 1.0. Evergreen: the schedule described is a standing calendar, not a report on any particular release.</p>
    <p>Companion dossier: <a href="/dossiers/gdp/">Dossier 02 · National Accounts</a>.</p>
  </div>

</Learn>
